From 10da5f6daabc4220cdc5fa15349958f0eea65987 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 10:14:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PS4 dynarmic: leaner block structure and register use; the
 profiler's block map

Mario Kart 8 Deluxe runs the A32 translator, which ended a block before every conditional
instruction, a conditional branch included: a loop's "cmp; bne" was two linked blocks a turn.
ARM's B<c> now ends the block it is in with an If terminal, as Thumb's B<c> already did.

- RegAlloc::MoveOutOfTheWay moves a value to an empty register (callee-saved first; during
  HostCall only those) instead of spilling it to the stack, which UseScratch of a value used
  again later, and every fixed-register scratch (lahf's rax, shifts' rcx), did.
- jit_frame_pointer (default on, off in Eden): the per-block %rbp save, frame setup and restore,
  there for host frame-pointer walks.
- link_forward_without_halt_check (off; on in Eden): a link to a higher address than the block's
  start skips the halt check; every loop links back at or below its start, which still checks.
- inline_rsb_pop (off; on in Eden): a return checks the RSB and jumps in its own block instead of
  through the shared handler's one indirect jump.

On the host harness with the PS4's CPU features (game-like image, 200 frames): A32 632k -> 472k
host instructions a frame, A64 513k -> 447k. Cross-build differential tests (old build vs new,
every option on, with and without fastmem), all passing: random A32/Thumb control flow and flags
(new generator) 1,700 programs, loads/stores, VFP lists and exclusives 1,400; A64 control flow
(new generator) 950, loads/stores 550, exclusives 200.

The PS4 profiler's map (backend/x64/ps4_block_map.cpp, PS4 builds, kept only while the launcher
enables it): each code cache's blocks in address order (host offset, sizes, guest location), for
the sampler's ps4_jit_block (the guest block of a JIT sample), ps4_jit_dump_function (the
hottest blocks' guest words and host bytes, to hotblocks.log) and ps4_crash_jit_info. Guest words
come from a new callback, PeekCode, side-effect free (Eden: GetPointerSilent), since
MemoryReadCode keeps a per-core cached page the sampler thread must not touch.
